% c552 (lens 2, A&S Units 9-10 PDF 135, 139 Ex. 9-8a, 141): 266 beat 3 ii(dim)6 as at 26 (c462): Cb A, Vla C4, Vln II B4-A4, Hn 3/Vc tenor A3 held over the bar as the prepared seventh (was G bass, G3 tenor, Vln II G4-G4, Vla B3-B3).
% c468 (lens 8, MOLA PDF 8 and Ex. 3/6): tenor countermelody swell 110-111/310-311 in every doubling part (Bsn 1, Hn 3, Hn 4 at 110), clarinet accents at 281, timpani pp restated at 367.
% c452 (lens 2, A&S Unit 5 PDF 76, 81-82, 87-88; Ex. 5-11b, 5-12, 5-29): the applied leading tone in the bass now rises a semitone in its own register into the hemiola 6/4 (cellos C sharp 3-D3 at 52-53, was a falling major seventh to D2; A sharp an octave lower at 128/284 in Vc/Bsn 2 A sharp 2 and Cb/Cbsn A sharp 1, was A sharp 3/A sharp 2 falling a major seventh to B). The bass meets the tune falling to 1 in contrary motion, and the falling sixth F sharp-A sharp turns back by step (PDF 81).
% c451 (lens 1, A&S Unit 20 PDF 334 Ex. 20-8, PDF 335-336 neighbour figure, PDF 340 Ex. 20-24): the second idea's cadential phrase opens with an upper neighbour and a passing tone instead of the arpeggio (G: d'''4 e'''8 d''' c'''4 was d'''4 b''4 g''4 at 53; E minor: b''4 cis'''8 b'' a''4 was b''4 g''4 e''4 at 129 and 285), so the top line falls 5-4-3-2-1 by step into the hemiola's 3-2-1 instead of reaching 1 a bar early; the bar takes motive x's rhythm, legato. Raised 6 (C sharp) over V in minor (Ex. 20-24; no C-E-G, c091). Ob 1 at 129/285 takes the upper octave from the downbeat (was B4 leaping to G5 on beat 2). Tenor doubling at 285 (Vc, Cl 2, Hn 2) keeps the frame tones (Ex. 20-27 footnote).
% c448 (lens 8, A&S Unit 3 PDF 51, 55, Ex. 3-6, 3-16-18): the second idea's cadential hemiola (54-55, 130-131, 286-287) now swells into the tied 9-8
%   (\< over the hemiola's first minim, peak on the dissonant downbeat, \> to the resolution on beat 2), in every voice that carries the tune (Vln I/II, Fl 1, Ob 1;
%   reprise also Vc, Cl 1/2, Hn 2 [Hn 2 shape only, level kept at f]). c441 turned the cadence into a suspension; every other melodic suspension in the movement
%   is shaded this way (T 155-156, 159-160, 165-166, 199-200; c348 row 13, c398). Hidden levels close each hairpin for the MIDI (c134).
% c412 (A&S Unit 25 sec. 4 and 6, PDF 422, 424, Ex. 25-12a; PDF 419 sec. 1; PDF 425 Ex. 25-14): 283-284 the E-minor second idea reaches its hemiola 6/4 through VII°7 of V,
%   A sharp-C sharp-E-G over the bass's rising half step A sharp-B (was V4/3 -> i6, the leading tone D sharp leaping to B in the four top-line instruments). The tune's bar 8 is now
%   D sharp-C sharp-B with the quaver anticipating the hemiola's B, as the G-major statement's F sharp-E-D at 52 over V6/5 of V. Cl 1 F sharp 4-G4 (the 7th G, held into the 6/4 at 285), Bsn 1 F sharp 3-C sharp 4-B3; Cb, Cbsn, Bsn 2 F sharp-A sharp-B; tenor octave (Vc, Hn 2, Cl 2) D sharp 4-C sharp 4-B3.
% c411: second idea, first minim of each phrase (45, 49, 121, 125, 277, 281): dotted crotchet + passing quaver inside the minim (d'''4. c'''8 b''4~ was d'''2 b''4~), filling the chordal third before the rising sixth. The tie over the bar line and the three-minim hemiola stay (attacks still on beats 1, 3, 2); the quaver is unaccented, as the lead-on quaver at 48/52 (Sebesky PDF 210, Ex. 277-278: a long-value lead line needs a figure of its own; PDF 206, Ex. 270: a recurring figure unifies).
% c339: 277-280 the second idea's antecedent in Fl 1 (at the violins' pitch) and Ob 1 (an octave lower), sub. p; the violins rest and take over
%   with the up-beat D on the last quaver of 280, the flute's last note (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 119: the same idea scored differently; PDF 125-126: the last note of
%   one part coincides with the first of the next). 121 gives it to the strings first and the winds join at 125; 277 reverses the order.
% c318: 277 Vln I/II up-bow (was down-bow) and 284 Vln I/II and violas end the slur before the quaver / on 284 beat 1, so 277-288 bow as 121-132 (c118, c218): sub. p at the light end of the bow, crescendo 281 on an up-bow, hemiola 285-287 on down-bows in violins and violas (Adler PDF 38).
% c268: 268 timpani p printed, as at 260 (MOLA PDF 10, Ex. 3).
% c218: 283-284 violas' repeated B under the slur as louré, as at 127 and 286 (Adler PDF 42).
% c175: 253-271 violas pizz. without staccato dots, as Vln II (same off-beats) and every other pizz. in the score (Adler PDF 54-55, Ex. II-55: pizz. notes plain); c018 kept the dots only because removing them gave a false analyser parallel at 263, which the current analyser no longer reports.
% c175: 271 Vln II beat 3 a rest (was G4 pizz.): five beats from the last pluck to the arco f entry at 273, as the violas have (Adler PDF 54: allow time for pizz. to arco, the harder change); the violas keep B3 on beat 3.
% c143: theme A bar 10 (22, 114, 262): the bass neighbour A-G-A under iv becomes A-G sharp-A, V6 of iv (E major over G sharp) instead of i6 (A-S Unit 18 PDF 297: in minor the neighbouring I6 to IV is "generally altered to form an applied dominant, V6 of IV"; a bass neighbour works best by half step). The leading tone G sharp is only in the bass line (A-S PDF 293); inner parts take B or E. 314 is unchanged: its G is passing (F sharp-G-A, c058).
% SCHERZO RETURNS, bars 253-288 (Tempo I). (c033: section-header bar numbers in dA, dB, dC brought to the v24+ numbering; c0NN tags keep their own cycle's numbers.)
% c085: 272 Vln II/Vla rest (5 beats for pizz.->arco at 273); 276 Vc rests, Bsn 2 keeps the bass D sharp 3 (5 beats for pizz.->arco at 277; Adler PDF 54, c018 rule). 261-268 Vc slur split 4+4 bars (Adler PDF 38-39: no phrase slurs in strings). 285-287 hemiola rebowed as 53-55.
% c035: 253 Fl 1, 269 Ob 1 and Bn 1 print "1."; 276 Ob 1·2 apart for one bar: the unison crotchet shares a notehead with two stems (Gould PDF 65) instead of the crowded "a2 1." label.
% c022: 263 viola B (was D sharp); 285-286 bass C-B, Bsn 1 E-D sharp: iv6-V (as sC 111, 125-126).
% 253-268: theme A as at 13, but Vln II pizzicato and Fl 1 doubling the violins heterophonically two octaves up (pp, with turns and passing notes).
% c549: Fl 1's heterophony starts at 261 (second phrase), with Hn 3's entry on the cellos' line (c449): the reprise's first phrase is Vln I's leggiero alone
%   over the cellos, then each phrase adds a layer (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 140-141: doubling only in isolated phrases, not incessantly; Ex. 289-290, 303: each phrase
%   of a returning line gets a new colour; PDF 146, Ex. 295: layers added one at a time). Cue Vln I 257-260 in the flute part (parts-cues.json).
% 269-276: the counterstatement compressed: Bsn 1 calls staccato with the cor anglais holding B (269-270; c042), the strings answer in E minor on V (271-272 = 31-32), then 273-275 = 41-43 at pitch (D7, subtonic seventh; c052) and 276 V6/5 of E, f (Vln II arco).
% c103: header line above corrected (it still said Ob 1 at 269 and '41-44 a minor third lower: V7 of E', the state before c042 and c052).
% c052: 273-275 at the exposition's pitch (41-43, D7 = subtonic seventh, heard as V7 of G as at 45), then 276 V6/5 of E (D sharp in the cellos' and Bsn 2's own
%   D-D sharp, Bsn 1 C-B, Cl 1 holds the A into 277's G): A-S PDF 410-411 sec. 12, Ex. 24-18 (Handel: subtonic 7th extended, then V6/5, 'surprising and dramatic',
%   an alternative to extending V7 itself), PDF 420 (chromatic step kept in one voice). Before: four bars of B7 after B7 at 272.
% 277-288: the second idea in E minor, as at 121-132 (c003: the reprise stays in the tonic, as Dvorak 9/iii's return does; the G-major version is heard once, at 45).

% c034: 258 and 261 Fl 1 turns use the lower neighbour (A6-G sharp 6, A6-G6) instead of B6: the movement's B6 is kept for the coda peak (336),
%   and the flute's top fourth cannot be played pp (Adler I PDF 185-186; II PDF 152).
% c036: Fl 1 p, not pp: its line climbs to A6, the flute's top fourth, where soft dynamics and intonation are hardest to control (Adler PDF 174, 186)
% c037: 253-268 the cellos leave the basses' pizzicato downbeats and play an arco pp tenor line of chord tones in dotted minims (B-G-F sharp, G-E-A-G, E-G-C-A ...),
%   the only sustained voice under the leggiero theme, so the reprise is not the exposition's three-layer accompaniment again (Adler II PDF 205: Brahms,
%   Hungarian Dance 1, steady pizzicato bass with an independent cello part; PDF 209: Dvorak's sustained "harmonic counterpoint" under a soft melody;
%   PDF 213: the repeat given a different flavour; PDF 230-231: a countertheme of the bar's own pitches in its own rhythm). Pizz. again at 271.
% c039: 277-288 the second idea's third statement (after 45 in G and 121 in E minor, both f) starts sub. p and crescendos from 281 to f at the hemiola 285,
%   instead of repeating 121-132 note for note at f (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 118: variety by 'variation of the general dynamic scheme'; PDF 127: amplification).
% c044: 277-288 the third statement of the second idea adds its doublings as it grows: violins alone in octaves at sub. p (277), Ob 1 joins the lower octave
%   with the crescendo (281), Fl 1 the upper octave at the f hemiola (285). 45 and 121 had Fl 1 and Ob 1 from the first note. Adler II PDF 172 (Meistersinger:
%   the highs held back, the crescendo built as upper octaves join the violins), PDF 170-171 (a recurring passage coloured differently each time).
% c134: 265 hidden p and Vln I hidden mp closing the swell 263-264 (MIDI only, prints nothing): with a bare \! the flute's hairpins ran in the MIDI to its next printed
%   dynamic (f at 273), so the render played the heterophony at velocity 58-62 (mf) for 265-268 over Vln I at 53-57, the subsidiary voice in its piercing register above
%   the principal one (Adler II PDF 184: principal louder, piercing subsidiary pp; PDF 162-163: a soft high flute sounds louder than it should; c069).
% c181: 263 Fl 1 beat 3 F sharp 6-E6 quavers (was F sharp 6 crotchet): the F sharp 6, a tritone above the cellos' and violas' C4, was left by a falling
%   diminished fifth to C6 at 264; now G-F sharp fill the fourth A6-E6 by step and E6 (chord tone of iv) falls a third to C6 (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 333 Ex. 20-10,
%   PDF 336: a dissonance followed by a leap is emphasized; PDF 343 points 3 and 6).
