% c447 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 113: strings and brass are seldom combined, except 'the splendid effect of horns doubled by divided violas or 'cellos'; PDF 112: octave and unison doublings strengthen one another):
%   253-268 Hn 3 doubles the cellos' tenor countermelody in unison, ppp dolce (a level under the cellos' pp, as c431's Hn 3 under the cellos at 169), so the line returns in the horn-and-cello colour it had at 105 (Hn 3-4 a2 + Vc) and has again at 305 (Hn 3 + Vc + Bn 1),
%   instead of the cellos alone; answers c161 (the horns are not saved here: Hn 1 sounds at 241-244; one horn pp in its middle register keeps the theme leggiero over the pizz. off-beats).
% c449 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 140-141: 'incessant duplication for an extended period of time should be avoided; it is only permissible in isolated phrases ... Doubling, when limited to a few special phrases supports the voice and endows it with beauty and colour'; Ex. 289-290, PDF 467-468: the cellos double the line 'dolce (colla voce)' for one phrase, the cor anglais takes the next; Ex. 303, PDF 491: Ob+Vln, then Cl+Vln):
