% c274: 281-287 Cl 2 joins Hn 2 and the cellos on the tenor octave of the second idea (it had doubled the violas' held notes): strings and brass
%   side by side are each heard too distinctly; the three timbres in unison give a rich, mellow, coherent tone (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 52); 288 Cl 2 B3, the chord's fifth.
% c071: 281-287 the second idea's last statement adds a tenor octave in Hn 2 and the cellos (C4-A3-F sharp 4 ..., an octave under Vln II and Ob 1):
%   Adler II PDF 27 (ch. 11, 'usually the horn and cello double on long sustained melodies', Strauss Ex. XI-10); PDF 20 (Hindemith: a later
%   statement of the theme takes a colour saved for it). The basses keep Cb, Bsn 2 and Cbsn; Hn 2 leaves its pad (Hn 1, Cl 1, violas keep the harmony).
% c359: 253-268 the cellos play the tenor countermelody of 105-120 at pp (same bass and theme, so the same harmony), so that at 305 the tutti brings back a line already heard alone (Adler I PDF 142-143, Ex. V-20: foreground and middleground introduced earlier without the background; Bruckner 6/iii combines its motives). 258 holds A3 instead of tying C4 into 259: under the pizz. bass a C4 at the end of 258 (the violas' pizz. C4 or a tied cello C4) read as the bass against the flute's G6-F sharp 6 (analyser fifths).
